        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started by ignoring the square-footage claims on the box. Heater makers quote coverage for a mild climate and a well-insulated room, and a garage is rarely either. Instead we worked out what each heater could realistically hold in three test cases: an insulated attached two-car garage, an uninsulated attached two-car garage, and a detached, uninsulated three-car shop, each with a 40 to 60 degree temperature rise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Output was the first filter. Anything that runs on a standard 120V outlet tops out at 1,500 watts, about 5,100 BTU per hour, which is enough to warm your hands at a bench and nowhere near enough to heat a two-car garage. We left the entire 120V category off this list for that reason. Every electric pick here runs on 240V.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next came installation reality. For each heater we checked the amperage draw and worked out the breaker and wire size it actually needs, not the one most buyers assume. For the gas heaters we checked venting category, mounting clearance and whether the unit needs a separate wall thermostat. A heater that is cheap to buy but needs a panel upgrade is not a budget heater.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then we looked at controls and placement, because a ceiling heater with a thermostat dial on the housing gets set once and forgotten. Remotes and wall thermostats earned credit. So did mounting that keeps the heater high, away from the fuel vapors that collect near a garage floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we weighed safety and moisture. Unvented combustion heaters put carbon monoxide and water vapor into the space, and that matters for rust, for floor coatings and for anyone in the room. Where we recommend one, we say exactly when it is appropriate.
          </p>
