        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How to Size a Garage Heater
          </h2>

          <p>
            Undersizing is the most common garage heater mistake, and it happens because people size by the number on the box. A reliable rule of thumb is to start with the floor area, apply a watts-per-square-foot figure that reflects your insulation, then adjust for how cold your winters are. Every watt of electric heat equals about 3.41 BTU per hour, so you can convert between electric and gas figures easily.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a garage with 8 to 10 foot ceilings and a 40 degree temperature rise, use about 10 watts, or 34 BTU per hour, per square foot if the walls, ceiling and door are insulated. Use 12 to 15 watts per square foot if the walls are insulated but the door is not, which describes most attached garages. Use 20 watts or more per square foot, about 68 BTU per hour, if the building is uninsulated. At that level electric heat gets expensive quickly and gas becomes the sensible choice.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Garage</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Insulated</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Walls Only</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Uninsulated</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">One-car, 280 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,800W / 9,600 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4,200W / 14,300 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5,600W / 19,100 BTU</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-car, 576 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5,800W / 19,700 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8,600W / 29,500 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">11,500W / 39,300 BTU</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Three-car, 864 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8,600W / 29,500 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">13,000W / 44,200 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">17,300W / 59,000 BTU</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The temperature rise, or delta-T, is the difference between your coldest typical outdoor temperature and the temperature you want inside. Heat loss scales roughly in proportion to it. If you want 55 degrees in a Minneapolis garage when it is minus 10 outside, that is a 65 degree rise, so multiply the table figures by about 1.6. Holding 50 degrees in Nashville when it is 20 outside is only a 30 degree rise, so you can scale down by a quarter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ceiling height matters too. A 12-foot ceiling holds 50 percent more air than an 8-foot one, and warm air rises into it. Add 10 to 20 percent for tall ceilings, and add a margin if you open the big door often, because every time you do, you are reheating the space from scratch.
          </p>
        </div>

