        <div id="fuel-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Electric vs Gas vs Infrared vs Portable
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are four realistic ways to heat a garage, and the right one depends mostly on what is already in the building. A 240V electric unit heater is the simplest permanent option: no venting, no combustion, no fuel to store, and nothing but a circuit to install. It is ideal for insulated garages up to about 1,000 square feet. Its weakness is cost per BTU in regions with expensive electricity and the size of the circuit it needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            A vented natural gas or propane unit heater, such as the Modine Hot Dawg or Mr. Heater Big Maxx, delivers far more heat for the money and is the right answer for uninsulated garages, large shops and cold climates. It needs a gas supply, a vent through the wall or roof, a 115V connection for the fan and controls, and usually a licensed installer. Once it is in, it runs for years with little attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            Radiant infrared heaters, including the gas tube heaters used in commercial shops and the electric panel or quartz heaters sold for garages, heat objects and people rather than air. That makes them effective in drafty or uninsulated spaces and in garages where the door is up constantly, because the heat does not escape with the air. They do less to warm the whole space, so if you need the garage itself above freezing, a forced-air heater does that job better. Note that some brands use the word infrared in their name while selling fan-forced heaters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Portable heaters, whether propane like the Big Buddy or kerosene and forced-air torpedo heaters, are for occasional use. The propane radiant type is indoor-rated with an oxygen depletion sensor; torpedo heaters generally are not intended for occupied, enclosed spaces. All unvented combustion heaters add moisture and carbon monoxide to the room.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Insulated and under 1,000 sq ft: 240V electric. Uninsulated, big or very cold: vented gas. Door always open: radiant. Once a month: portable propane.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Pick the fuel first, then the model. Most unhappy garage heater owners bought a good heater of the wrong type for their building.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="wiring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Wiring a 240V Garage Heater
          </h2>

          <p>
            The amperage math is simple, and getting it wrong is the most common reason a new garage heater trips its breaker or never gets installed at all. Divide watts by volts to get amps: 5,000 watts at 240V is 20.8 amps, 7,500 watts is 31.25 amps, and 10,000 watts is 41.7 amps. Because a fixed heater is a continuous load, the circuit is sized at 125 percent of that figure, and the breaker and wire must be rated for it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Heater</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Amps at 240V</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Breaker</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Copper Wire</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20.8A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30A double-pole</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 AWG</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5,600W plug-in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23.3A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30A with 6-30R outlet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 AWG</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">7,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">31.3A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40A double-pole</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8 AWG</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">10,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">41.7A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60A double-pole</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 AWG</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That table shows a point many garage heater guides get wrong: a 30-amp circuit is right for 5,000-watt heaters, but a 7,500-watt heater needs a 40-amp circuit. The 125 percent rule turns 31.25 amps into about 39 amps of required capacity. Always confirm against the installation manual for your specific model, which lists the minimum circuit ampacity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before any of that, check that your panel has room. A 7,500-watt heater running for hours on a winter evening, alongside an electric range, dryer and water heater, can push a 100-amp service beyond what it can supply. An electrician can run a load calculation in a few minutes and tell you whether you need a subpanel or a service upgrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hardwired heaters also need a disconnect within sight or a lockable breaker, and many include a built-in thermostat that switches the full load. For plug-in 240V heaters like the DR-988, note that recent electrical code editions extend GFCI protection to 240V garage receptacles in many jurisdictions. If your area has adopted the 2020 or later code, a new 6-30 outlet in the garage will likely need a GFCI breaker. Pull a permit; it is cheap insurance on a circuit this size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="venting-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Venting, CO and Garage Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            A garage is not a living room, and it has specific hazards that change how heaters must be installed. The biggest is fuel vapor. Gasoline vapor is heavier than air and settles near the floor, so building codes generally require any appliance with an ignition source in a residential garage to be elevated at least 18 inches above the floor. Ceiling-mounted heaters clear that easily. Floor-standing heaters do not, which is one reason we prefer ceiling units.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vented gas heaters send their combustion products outdoors. The Mr. Heater Big Maxx vents vertically as a Category I appliance through the roof or horizontally as a Category III appliance through a sidewall, while the Modine Hot Dawg uses a built-in power venter. Either way, the vent must use the pipe type the manual specifies, maintain its clearances from windows and doors, and terminate where snow cannot block it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unvented heaters are different. Portable propane heaters with oxygen depletion sensors are rated for indoor use, but they still release carbon monoxide at low levels plus a lot of water vapor. In an attached garage that air can find its way into the house through the connecting door. Crack a window or the big door a few inches while one is running, and never leave it on unattended.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every garage with a combustion heater, and every home with an attached garage, should have a carbon monoxide alarm. Put one in the garage and one in the room on the other side of the connecting door. They are inexpensive and they are the only warning you will get.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, respect clearances. Electric unit heaters have minimum mounting heights, typically 6 feet or more, and clearances to walls and ceilings. Do not aim a heater at a car, a gas can or a pile of rags, and keep the space in front of it clear of lumber and storage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What a Garage Heater Costs to Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running cost is where gas and electric heaters differ most, and it is worth doing the math with your own utility rates before you commit. Electric heat is 100 percent efficient at the heater, but electricity is an expensive way to buy heat in most of the country. Gas unit heaters lose some heat up the vent, yet natural gas usually costs a fraction as much per BTU.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is a simple worked example using round rates you can swap for your own. A 7,500-watt heater running flat out for an hour uses 7.5 kilowatt-hours. At 15 cents per kilowatt-hour, that is about $1.13 an hour. A 45,000 BTU input gas heater uses 0.45 therms an hour, and at $1.20 per therm that is about 54 cents, while delivering more heat. Propane at around 91,500 BTU per gallon costs more than natural gas in most areas, so do that calculation separately.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">ONE HOUR AT FULL OUTPUT, EXAMPLE RATES</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">7.5 kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">7,500W electric heater</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0.45 therm</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">45,000 BTU gas heater</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3,412</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">BTU per kilowatt-hour</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">100,000</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">BTU per therm of gas</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Real usage is far lower than full output, because a thermostat cycles the heater once the space is up to temperature. How much lower depends almost entirely on insulation and on how often the door is opened. An insulated garage held at 50 degrees might run its heater a quarter of the time on a cold day; an uninsulated one with the door up frequently might run it continuously.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cheapest strategy is a low set point when you are not there, 40 to 45 degrees to protect paint, glue and water-based products from freezing, and a higher one only while you are working. A remote or wall thermostat makes that easy. A heater with the dial on the housing tends to stay at whatever temperature it was set to in November.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="epoxy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Heating a Garage for Floor Projects
          </h2>

          <p>
            One of the best reasons to heat a garage has nothing to do with comfort. Floor coatings are temperature-sensitive, and most garage floor epoxies need both the air and the concrete slab at 50 to 60 degrees or warmer to cure properly. Below that, the chemical reaction slows dramatically, the coating stays soft for days, and adhesion and hardness suffer. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-apply-garage-floor-epoxy/">how to apply garage floor epoxy</a> covers the full process, but the heating part is easy to overlook.
          </p>

          <p>
            The slab is the problem, not the air. A concrete floor holds cold for a long time, and warm air above it does not mean the surface is warm. If you want to coat a floor in autumn or early spring, start heating the garage at least 24 to 48 hours before you apply the first coat, and keep the heat running through the cure time the manufacturer lists. An inexpensive infrared thermometer pointed at the slab tells you whether you are there yet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the right kind of heat. Unvented propane and kerosene heaters add water vapor and carbon dioxide to the air, and both can react with the curing agent in epoxy to leave a waxy, cloudy film called amine blush. Vented gas or electric heat avoids that. That is also why we would not rely on a portable propane heater for the job, however convenient it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are still deciding on a floor, our round-ups of <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">the best garage floor epoxy</a> and <a href="/best-epoxy-garage-floor-kit/">complete epoxy garage floor kits</a> list the temperature windows for each product. Interlocking tiles are far less fussy about temperature, and <a href="/best-garage-floor-tiles/">our garage floor tile picks</a> are worth a look if you cannot keep the slab warm. The broader comparison in <a href="/garage-flooring-options/">garage flooring options</a> covers cost and durability for each choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="insulate-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Tighten the Building Before You Upsize
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every dollar spent sealing a garage buys a smaller heater and a lower bill for as long as you own it. Before you jump from a 7,500-watt heater to a 10,000-watt one, look at where the heat is going, because in most garages the answer is the big door and the ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            The garage door is usually the weakest part of the envelope. An uninsulated steel door is effectively a single sheet of metal, and the gaps at the bottom and sides can add up to several square feet of opening. Replacing the bottom seal and the side and top weatherstripping is inexpensive and fast. Insulation kits made for garage door panels add foam or reflective board and make a noticeable difference in how quickly the space loses heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, the ceiling. If there is an unheated attic above the garage, adding insulation there often matters more than insulating the walls, because warm air rises and a heater pushes it straight up. Seal the attic hatch or ladder opening too; it is often a bare panel with gaps all round.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then deal with air leaks and water. Caulk gaps where the walls meet the slab and around windows and service doors. Check the gutters above the garage door; an overflowing or leaking gutter seam drips water right at the door threshold, where it refreezes into an ice ridge that stops the seal closing properly. Our guide to <a href="/best-gutter-sealant/">the best gutter sealant</a> covers fixing those leaks for good.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, reduce the delta-T you are asking for. If you only need to protect stored items from freezing, 40 degrees is enough. If you work out there in a jacket, 50 to 55 degrees is comfortable. Heating a garage to 68 degrees is rarely necessary and costs far more than the extra comfort is worth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Mounting and Placement
          </h2>

          <p>
            Where a unit heater hangs affects how well it works as much as its output does. The goal is to move warm air across the working area and down to the floor, rather than letting it pool against the ceiling where it does nothing useful.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mount ceiling heaters in a corner and aim them along the long axis of the garage, angled down toward the floor at roughly 45 degrees. That lets the air travel the full length of the space and wash across the floor on its way back. Pointing a heater straight at the opposite wall from a short distance wastes most of its throw distance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep heaters away from the path of the garage door and its opener, and above the minimum mounting height in the manual. Most electric unit heaters need about 6 feet from the floor to the bottom of the heater, and gas unit heaters often need 8 feet. In garages with low ceilings, that rules some models out before you even consider output.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about where you actually stand. If you spend most of your time at a bench on one wall, aim the heater so that its airflow reaches you. If you use the garage for parking and a small amount of work, aim it at the car bay so it clears frost and melts snow off the vehicle. Moving the thermostat or sensor away from the heater outlet also helps the whole space reach a more even temperature.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, give the heater room to breathe. Keep the intake clear of stored items, vacuum dust and sawdust off the grilles at the start of each season, and do not hang anything from the heater bracket. A clean heater runs cooler and lasts longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 120V heater for a two-car garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A standard outlet delivers at most 1,500 watts, about 5,100 BTU per hour. That is spot heat for one person at a bench, not heat for a garage. If you want the space itself warm, plan on a 240V electric heater or a vented gas unit heater from the start rather than buying twice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a 7,500-watt heater on a 30-amp circuit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 7,500-watt heater draws 31.25 amps at 240V, and a heater is a continuous load sized at 125 percent. That means a 40-amp circuit with 8-gauge copper. A 30-amp breaker will trip, and worse, undersized wire will run hot. Check the minimum circuit ampacity in the manual before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing from the box instead of the building</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The coverage claim on the box assumes a mild climate and good insulation. An uninsulated garage in a cold region can need double the output. Measure the floor area, apply a watts-per-square-foot figure for your insulation, then scale for your coldest typical outdoor temperature before choosing a model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running an unvented heater while curing epoxy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Portable propane and kerosene heaters release water vapor and carbon dioxide that can react with epoxy hardener and leave a cloudy amine blush. Use electric or vented gas heat to bring the slab to temperature, and keep combustion heaters out of the garage until the coating has fully cured.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the carbon monoxide alarm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Any combustion heater in an attached garage can put carbon monoxide into the house through the connecting door, especially if a vent is blocked or the heater is poorly adjusted. A CO alarm in the garage and another inside the house is an inexpensive precaution that should come before the heater, not after.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size heater do I need for a two-car garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical two-car garage is about 576 square feet. If it is insulated, a 7,500-watt electric heater, about 25,600 BTU per hour, is a comfortable fit in most climates, and 5,000 watts is marginal. If it is uninsulated or you live somewhere very cold, plan on a 40,000 to 50,000 BTU vented gas heater instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a gas or electric garage heater cheaper to run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most areas natural gas is considerably cheaper per unit of heat than electricity, even after accounting for heat lost up the vent. Electric heaters cost less to buy and install if you have panel capacity, so they often make more sense in insulated garages used a few times a week. Use your own utility rates to compare.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug a garage heater into a regular outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can plug in a 1,500-watt 120V heater, but it will only warm a small area near you. Real garage heaters run on 240V. Some, like the Dr. Infrared DR-988, plug into a 30-amp NEMA 6-30 outlet, but most ceiling units must be hardwired to a dedicated double-pole breaker.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What breaker does a 7,500-watt heater need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At 240V a 7,500-watt heater draws 31.25 amps. Heaters are continuous loads, so the circuit is sized at 125 percent, about 39 amps. That puts you on a 40-amp double-pole breaker with 8-gauge copper wire in most cases. Confirm against the minimum circuit ampacity printed in your heater's installation manual.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use a propane heater in an attached garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An indoor-rated propane heater with an oxygen depletion sensor, such as the Mr. Heater Big Buddy, can be used in a garage with ventilation. Crack a window or the door a few inches, keep a carbon monoxide alarm running, store spare cylinders outside and never leave it running unattended or while anyone is sleeping nearby.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How warm should I keep my garage in winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                To protect paint, adhesives, batteries and water-based products from freezing, 40 to 45 degrees is enough. For working comfortably in a jacket, 50 to 55 degrees is usually fine. Holding a garage at living-room temperature costs far more than it is worth, so use a low set point when nobody is out there.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to heat my garage to apply floor epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, if it is cold. Most garage floor epoxies need the slab and air at 50 to 60 degrees or warmer to cure properly. Heat the space for a day or two beforehand so the concrete warms through, check the slab with an infrared thermometer, and use electric or vented gas heat rather than an unvented propane heater.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

