        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Size the heater to the building, not the box. For most insulated two-car garages, the Dr. Infrared DR-975 at 7,500 watts is the best balance of output, controls and installation cost, provided you run the 40-amp circuit it actually needs. If your garage is uninsulated, large or somewhere with hard winters, a vented gas unit heater like the Modine Hot Dawg HD45 or the less expensive Mr. Heater Big Maxx will heat it properly for less money per hour.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you choose, install a carbon monoxide alarm, keep ignition sources off the floor, and spend a little on weatherstripping the big door before you spend a lot on a bigger heater. A tighter garage with a right-sized heater is warmer and cheaper to run than a drafty one with an oversized unit.
          </p>
